WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:04.280
All right.

00:04.280 --> 00:05.720
So now we're going to move to the second type.

00:05.720 --> 00:11.600
And we're gonna understand how exactly a SQL build and create the non-clustered index.

00:11.600 --> 00:12.280
So let's go.

00:12.280 --> 00:16.400
So now we are back to the heap structure where our table don't have any index.

00:16.400 --> 00:19.640
And our data are stored randomly inside the data pages.

00:19.640 --> 00:24.720
And now if you go and create a non-clustered index on the customer ID what can happen.

00:24.720 --> 00:32.880
And here the big difference is that SQL will not touch or change anything on the physical, actual data

00:32.880 --> 00:34.280
on the data pages.

00:34.280 --> 00:38.600
So the data pages can stay as it is and nothing's going to be changed.

00:38.640 --> 00:42.280
The and the SQL start immediately building the B structure.

00:42.280 --> 00:45.280
So it's going to start immediately building an index page.

00:45.280 --> 00:49.360
And this index page is a little bit different than the one that we have learned previously.

00:49.360 --> 00:52.200
So since it is index page it's going to store pointers.

00:52.200 --> 00:56.000
But this time it's really going to store in the key the customer ID.

00:56.000 --> 00:59.760
So one is the customer ID and now the value the pointer.

00:59.760 --> 01:03.360
It will not be the data page ID we will be more specific.

01:03.360 --> 01:08.160
So we're going to have like an address where exactly the row is stored.

01:08.160 --> 01:14.010
So it's going to start with the file ID the page number because the customer ID one is stored in the

01:14.010 --> 01:22.490
page one double point 102, but SQL can go add as well the offset number of the row where exactly in

01:22.490 --> 01:25.210
the page we can find this ID and the whole thing.

01:25.210 --> 01:29.130
We can call it an air ID the row identifier.

01:29.130 --> 01:35.210
So now let's see quickly how the index page is pointing exactly to the row inside the data page.

01:35.210 --> 01:40.530
So the first part of the row identifier is mapping to the data page ID.

01:40.530 --> 01:43.610
And then from the 96 it's going to take us to the offset.

01:43.610 --> 01:47.290
And that's exactly the location of the row number one.

01:47.290 --> 01:52.450
So 96 is the part where we're going to start finding the row number one.

01:52.450 --> 01:58.850
And that's going to take us exactly to the place where we can read the information about the row ID

01:58.890 --> 01:59.690
number one.

01:59.690 --> 02:05.370
So this is how the index page is locating the exact place of the rows.

02:05.410 --> 02:12.330
So SQL can go and continue and assign for each customer ID a pointer to the exact location.

02:12.330 --> 02:18.050
So as you can see now in the index page, we don't have like a pointer for each group of customers,

02:18.050 --> 02:24.570
like we have learned in the clusters index, we have now a pointer for each ID and this type of index

02:24.570 --> 02:27.410
page, we call it row locator page.

02:27.410 --> 02:33.890
So now SQL going to go and continue and map a pointer for each customer ID that we have inside our table.

02:33.890 --> 02:38.090
So we will have multiple index pages pointing to our data page.

02:38.210 --> 02:40.370
So as you can see we have a lot of pointers.

02:40.530 --> 02:44.570
And the data inside the index page is of course sorted.

02:44.570 --> 02:47.690
But inside the data pages it left as it is.

02:47.850 --> 02:54.890
And now those index pages that has the row identifier is going to be stored at the leaf level of that

02:54.970 --> 02:55.690
B-tree.

02:55.730 --> 02:59.370
So at the leaf level we don't have the actual data, the data pages.

02:59.370 --> 03:04.050
We have index pages where we have pointers then to the actual data.

03:04.090 --> 03:07.410
And then it's going to go and start building the intermediate nodes.

03:07.410 --> 03:12.850
It's exactly like the clustered index where it's going to point to another index page.

03:12.850 --> 03:17.850
So between 1 and 5 customers it can be in the index page number 200.

03:17.850 --> 03:20.410
So the next step is going to go and build the intermediate nodes.

03:20.410 --> 03:23.050
It's going to be exactly like the clustered index.

03:23.090 --> 03:24.170
Nothing going to be changed.

03:24.210 --> 03:25.730
It's like the same structure.

03:25.730 --> 03:31.290
So it is an index page pointing to another index page, but this time for a group of customers.

03:31.290 --> 03:34.130
And then we're going to have as well the root node.

03:34.260 --> 03:40.140
So again, we call this structure as a B-tree structure where they point to another data pages.

03:40.140 --> 03:43.980
But the data pages are not part of the B tree.

03:44.020 --> 03:48.580
So now let's say if you are searching for the customer ID number 14, what's going to happen?

03:48.620 --> 03:51.020
It's going to start again from the root node.

03:51.140 --> 03:55.420
And then it's going to find the pointer to the intermediate node and then jump to the next step to the

03:55.420 --> 03:56.300
intermediate node.

03:56.300 --> 04:00.860
And then it's going to find the pointer to the index page between 11 and 15.

04:00.860 --> 04:04.780
And then it's going to go and scan this index page and find okay.

04:04.820 --> 04:08.620
For the customer ID number 14 we have the following address.

04:08.620 --> 04:11.140
So it's going to go and locate the exact data page.

04:11.140 --> 04:17.620
And as well the exact place of the row so it can go and jump immediately to the row without scanning

04:17.660 --> 04:18.540
anything else.

04:18.700 --> 04:24.820
So here this time with the non-clustered index, the SQL did read three different index pages.

04:24.820 --> 04:28.340
And finally the one data page in order to find the data.

04:28.340 --> 04:34.100
So if you compare to the clustered index you can see that we have here one extra layer, one extra index

04:34.100 --> 04:38.020
page to be scanned in order to find the right place of the row.

04:38.020 --> 04:45.100
And this is how SQL creates the B-tree for the non-clustered index and how it scans it in order to find

04:45.100 --> 04:46.100
the information.
